00:10:04.792 --> 00:10:05.625
- Michael I...

00:10:05.625 --> 00:10:06.708
- [Michael] Yeah, and I--
- Go ahead.

00:10:06.708 --> 00:10:08.125
- [Michael] I really
appreciate that,

00:10:08.125 --> 00:10:09.542
but for me, like, when I was

00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:11.125
in eighth grade my dad...

00:10:11.125 --> 00:10:13.166
I'm being so vulnerable
with you guys right now,

00:10:13.166 --> 00:10:16.166
eighth grade, my dad was
arrested, I had to sell my

00:10:16.166 --> 00:10:19.208
house, sell all the cars, I was
15 years old, and I had to

00:10:19.208 --> 00:10:22.000
get a job, help pay
the rent back at home.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.750
And I've been trying
to use that in my music,

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:27.375
and it's so cool, 'cause I
only have like, I don't know,

00:10:27.375 --> 00:10:30.375
2,000 followers on
Instagram or whatever but I get,

00:10:30.375 --> 00:10:33.333
direct message like once or
twice a week, just say I'm like,

00:10:33.333 --> 00:10:35.834
hey man, your music's
really inspiring to me, to me,

00:10:35.834 --> 00:10:39.417
that's crazy because I live
in D.C., and there's so many

00:10:39.417 --> 00:10:41.542
kids out there that look
up to these rappers that are

00:10:41.542 --> 00:10:43.542
talkin' about guns,
drugs, or whatever,

00:10:43.542 --> 00:10:45.917
but it's just like, I think
you can change the world if

00:10:45.917 --> 00:10:49.875
you can make music that is
inspiring kids to be educated.

00:10:49.875 --> 00:10:52.250
And stop the violence,
or whatever it may be.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:53.834
That's my goal with all of it,

00:10:53.834 --> 00:10:56.291
and John your music
has inspired me so much.

00:10:56.291 --> 00:10:58.875
"All of me", I might have
that play at my wedding.

00:10:58.875 --> 00:11:01.792
(Gary and John laugh)

00:11:01.792 --> 00:11:03.667
- Michael, listen, 
I think you're barkin' up

00:11:03.667 --> 00:11:05.291
the right tree.
Here's what I would say.

00:11:05.291 --> 00:11:07.875
Don't get impatient,
and don't get frustrated.

00:11:07.875 --> 00:11:11.458
If you speak your truth,
when at 29, you may

00:11:11.458 --> 00:11:12.667
break through right?

00:11:12.667 --> 00:11:14.667
Like, John, talk about
your, you know, coming...

00:11:14.667 --> 00:11:16.375
- My journey was--
- Yeah.

00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:17.834
- First of all, I started
playing the piano when I was

00:11:17.834 --> 00:11:19.000
four years old.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.875
I started singing publicly
when I was like, six or seven.

00:11:21.875 --> 00:11:25.417
So if you think about it, my
career has been 30, over 30

00:11:25.417 --> 00:11:28.166
years of performing
in front of people.

00:11:28.166 --> 00:11:30.917
I was doing it at a church,
I was doing it at school,

00:11:30.917 --> 00:11:33.041
and by the time
I got to college,

00:11:33.041 --> 00:11:34.542
I went there at sixteen,

00:11:34.542 --> 00:11:37.166
and I was singing in an
acapella group, and I was also

00:11:37.166 --> 00:11:39.959
directing a
church choir, and then,

00:11:39.959 --> 00:11:42.291
I wanted to get a
record deal at some point.

00:11:42.291 --> 00:11:45.083
And I wanted that since I was
a kid, but I started to see

00:11:45.083 --> 00:11:49.125
the path when I got to
college, and I started making

00:11:49.125 --> 00:11:51.708
my first demos
when I was like, 18.

00:11:51.708 --> 00:11:53.583
I started
writing a lot more songs,

00:11:53.583 --> 00:11:55.208
collaborating with more people,

00:11:55.208 --> 00:11:57.875
and I thought I was ready
to get a record deal then.

00:11:57.875 --> 00:12:00.208
You know, I had that same
exuberance that you have that,

00:12:00.208 --> 00:12:04.583
that you know, I'm great, why
isn't someone signing me yet?

00:12:04.583 --> 00:12:06.917
And it took me a while to
realize that I had to keep

00:12:06.917 --> 00:12:09.417
working at it, keep
getting better, keep writing,

00:12:09.417 --> 00:12:12.959
and eventually it worked out,
but it didn't work out until

00:12:12.959 --> 00:12:16.333
2004 when I was 25
going on 26 years old,

00:12:18.333 --> 00:12:21.166
So, it's a process, and
you have to be persistent,

00:12:21.166 --> 00:12:24.000
and you have to take
constructive criticism,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.875
and you have to realize
that even when people say no,

00:12:27.875 --> 00:12:29.458
that shouldn't
discourage you from

00:12:29.458 --> 00:12:31.333
keeping going and working hard.

00:12:31.333 --> 00:12:35.041
- Did your noes,
did they excite you?

00:12:35.041 --> 00:12:36.041
I mean, everybody's different.

00:12:36.041 --> 00:12:38.417
The reason I'm
asking you that, I love no.

00:12:38.417 --> 00:12:40.000
- They were frustrating, but...

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:43.417
- Wasn't it like, fuck you,
I'm going to win anyway?

00:12:43.417 --> 00:12:47.458
- It wasn't an active
fuck you, it was more like,

00:12:50.208 --> 00:12:52.417
I believed I could
prove them wrong.

00:12:52.417 --> 00:12:53.875
But it also made me think,

00:12:53.875 --> 00:12:55.542
what would I need
to do to improve myself?

00:12:55.542 --> 00:12:57.583
Because I think you can't
just think you know everything

00:12:57.583 --> 00:13:00.083
already, and you're already
great at everything already.

00:13:00.083 --> 00:13:03.291
Especially at your age,
like, there's a lot of things

00:13:03.291 --> 00:13:04.792
you gotta get better at.

00:13:04.792 --> 00:13:06.291
And if you're getting criticism,

00:13:06.291 --> 00:13:08.375
maybe some of
the people are right.

00:13:08.375 --> 00:13:09.750
Maybe they're not all right,

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.166
but maybe some
of them are right.

00:13:11.166 --> 00:13:14.125
And that doesn't mean stop,
that means work even harder,

00:13:14.125 --> 00:13:15.166
and get even better.

00:13:15.166 --> 00:13:17.500
- Michael, for you,
and everybody who is watching,

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:20.625
the key is self-awareness,
and doing it your way.

00:13:20.625 --> 00:13:22.166
For me, it was
really "fuck you."

00:13:22.166 --> 00:13:24.834
It was.
It was my way.

00:13:24.834 --> 00:13:27.583
I was talking about selling
stuff on the internet in the

00:13:27.583 --> 00:13:30.208
mid 90's, and it was
hard for people to buy in.

00:13:30.208 --> 00:13:32.792
Other people, it's, okay,
I'm going to prove you wrong.

00:13:32.792 --> 00:13:35.542
But what did I just hear from
you that could build on...

00:13:35.542 --> 00:13:38.250
I would say that one of my
weaknesses is, I don't take

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:40.834
the constructive criticism,
and factor it in enough.

00:13:40.834 --> 00:13:43.000
I'd rather run through the wall
and figure it out for myself

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.750
but that's because I know me,
and you know you, and so

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.291
I would say, Michael, you gotta
take different pieces, but

00:13:48.291 --> 00:13:52.291
patience, and knowing yourself
are two absolute fundamental

00:13:52.291 --> 00:13:55.458
formulas for this tough game.
Thanks for your call, man.

00:13:55.458 --> 00:13:57.333
- [Michael] (mumbling)
- Thank you, brother.

00:13:57.333 --> 00:13:58.208
- [Michael] Yeah,
real quick, Gary,

00:13:58.208 --> 00:14:00.542
can I ask you one last question?
- Go ahead.

00:14:00.542 --> 00:14:02.708
- [Michael] So,
one thing for John.

00:14:02.708 --> 00:14:04.834
John, I've never
talked to someone like this

00:14:04.834 --> 00:14:06.917
before in my life, so I'm just
going to throw it out there.

00:14:06.917 --> 00:14:10.041
If I make a dope track, would
you consider singing on it?

00:14:10.041 --> 00:14:11.417
- Who me or John?

00:14:13.792 --> 00:14:18.375
- It's gotta be
really dope! (laughs)

00:14:18.375 --> 00:14:21.792
- Like, the dopest of dope.
We're hanging it up after that.

00:14:21.792 --> 00:14:25.083
- Thanks Michael.
(John laughs)

00:14:25.083 --> 00:14:28.834
While Andy's getting the
next call, how do you think,

00:14:28.834 --> 00:14:31.083
I mean, that was very
nice of Michael, and obviously

00:14:31.083 --> 00:14:32.291
that's a Hail Mary,
and good for him.

00:14:32.291 --> 00:14:34.500
- Hey, why not?
It doesn't hurt to ask.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:37.500
- How often are you
being asked to sing the hook,

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.333
or like, collab...

00:14:39.333 --> 00:14:41.583
I mean, people that are big
deals, and are real celebrities

00:14:41.583 --> 00:14:43.417
I assume, are in that world...

00:14:43.417 --> 00:14:45.750
How often is that happening?

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:48.542
How do you even go
about thinking about that?

00:14:48.542 --> 00:14:51.417
- Well, the...

00:14:51.417 --> 00:14:53.542
It doesn't
get to me very often

00:14:53.542 --> 00:14:55.417
because I have enough people

00:14:55.417 --> 00:14:58.708
to kind of
filter through things.

00:14:58.708 --> 00:15:01.542
So the chances
that I'll get to meet

00:15:01.542 --> 00:15:04.041
are only when it's
really serious artists that

00:15:04.041 --> 00:15:05.708
are, you know, major level.

00:15:05.708 --> 00:15:06.875
- [Gary] It's big.
- Major people.

00:15:06.875 --> 00:15:08.250
- Have you ever
reached out to anybody?

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:09.333
Or like, oh my God,
I love this up

00:15:09.333 --> 00:15:11.166
and coming kid,
I wanna put them on?

00:15:11.166 --> 00:15:13.708
(phone ringing)
- Absolutely.

00:15:13.708 --> 00:15:17.667
Usually it's not I wanna
put them on, it's usually

00:15:17.667 --> 00:15:19.750
I've already worked
with them in some way.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.959
Maybe I've written
with them or they've shown

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:25.333
me in some way
they they're talented.

00:15:25.333 --> 00:15:26.750
- [Gary] Sharon, hold on.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.750
- And then I'll say,
why don't you

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:31.291
work with me on this track?

00:15:31.291 --> 00:15:32.500
And a lot of times
it's more behind the scenes,

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:33.667
they're writing
or they're playing

00:15:33.667 --> 00:15:35.000
guitar, they're
doing this or that.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.000
- [Gary] Got it.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.542
- And for me to use,
to reach out to someone

00:15:38.542 --> 00:15:40.500
and try to get them
as a featured artist on

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.917
my project,
usually they're a pretty

00:15:41.917 --> 00:15:43.041
established artist already.

00:15:43.041 --> 00:15:44.667
'Cause ya know, why?

00:15:44.667 --> 00:15:45.583
- [Gary] You're just,
you're at that place.

00:15:45.583 --> 00:15:47.125
- Yeah, I'm at that
place where you might as

00:15:47.125 --> 00:15:49.792
well have somebody
that not only adds something

00:15:49.792 --> 00:15:52.333
musically, but also
their marketing value.

00:15:52.333 --> 00:15:54.458
- [Gary] Of course, Sharon?

00:15:54.458 --> 00:15:55.708
- [Sharon] Yes, this is Sharon.

00:15:55.708 --> 00:15:56.625
- I don't know
if you've noticed,

00:15:56.625 --> 00:15:57.792
but this is
GaryVee and you're on

00:15:57.792 --> 00:16:00.125
The #AskGaryVee Show
with John Legend.

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:00.875
- Hi Sharon.

00:16:00.875 --> 00:16:03.625
- [Sharon] I know, 
hi there, I'm so excited!

00:16:03.625 --> 00:16:05.166
- Where you callin' from?

00:16:05.166 --> 00:16:06.375
- [Sharon] Everyone's
numbers scrolling past,

00:16:06.375 --> 00:16:09.291
I'm like, there's no
freaking way, no way.

00:16:09.291 --> 00:16:12.083
- Your number was
picked, where you from?

00:16:12.083 --> 00:16:14.250
- [Sharon] I'm
from Safford, Arizona.

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.083
- Very nice.

00:16:15.083 --> 00:16:17.083
- Arizona, I'll be
in Phoenix soon.

00:16:17.083 --> 00:16:19.333
- On tour?
- [Sharon] Oh, yeah, awesome!

00:16:19.333 --> 00:16:21.500
- You better go to the show.

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.917
- [Sharon] I know,
I'm like, where and when?

00:16:23.917 --> 00:16:25.583
Will I have my
kids that weekend?

00:16:25.583 --> 00:16:26.458
(laughing)

00:16:26.458 --> 00:16:27.291
- Take them!

00:16:27.291 --> 00:16:29.208
- JohnLegend.com,
you can bring your kids.

00:16:29.208 --> 00:16:30.750
(John laughs)

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:32.917
- Sharon, so
what's your question?

00:16:32.917 --> 00:16:35.708
- [Sharon] Well I mean
you're both such big.

00:16:35.708 --> 00:16:37.375
- Good looking guys!

00:16:38.375 --> 00:16:39.542
- [Sharon] Oh definitely.

00:16:39.542 --> 00:16:41.542
- Obviously that's
what Sharon's saying.

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:42.375
(Gary laughs)

00:16:42.375 --> 00:16:43.208
- [Sharon] You're
both strong forces

00:16:43.208 --> 00:16:45.333
in your industry,
how do you stay

00:16:45.333 --> 00:16:48.250
humble, yet strong,
you stay humble?

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:51.083
I know you stay humble, but
you're such strong forces.

00:16:51.083 --> 00:16:53.792
How do you balance that,
those two aspects?

00:16:53.792 --> 00:16:56.000
'Cause they're very
opposite because some

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.959
people come off cocky,
you know what I'm saying?

00:16:58.959 --> 00:16:59.792
- Yeah.

00:16:59.792 --> 00:17:02.375
- [Sharon] How do you do that?
How do you balance that?

00:17:02.375 --> 00:17:03.625
- Well I think you have to know

00:17:03.625 --> 00:17:04.583
what you know, and also be aware

00:17:04.583 --> 00:17:06.165
of what you
don't know, and I think

00:17:06.165 --> 00:17:08.791
part of being smart,
part of being savvy

00:17:08.791 --> 00:17:10.375
is knowing
where you might need

00:17:10.375 --> 00:17:12.916
someones help,
knowing where

00:17:12.916 --> 00:17:14.834
you're not the greatest
at everything and

00:17:14.834 --> 00:17:17.333
if you're confident
enough in yourself

00:17:17.333 --> 00:17:18.708
to ask for help at
the right time, it's like

00:17:18.708 --> 00:17:22.333
the right balance of
humility and confidence.

00:17:22.333 --> 00:17:23.750
Other people have
expertise in areas

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:25.416
that you don't
have them in, like Gary,

00:17:25.416 --> 00:17:27.708
has expertise in areas
that I don't have them in.

00:17:27.708 --> 00:17:30.917
- [Gary] And I can't play
the piano for shit.

00:17:30.917 --> 00:17:33.375
(Sharon laughs)
- And if I'm confident and also

00:17:33.375 --> 00:17:35.792
humble, humble
enough to say here's

00:17:35.792 --> 00:17:37.375
where I could
use some help,

00:17:37.375 --> 00:17:38.792
here's where
I could use some advice.

00:17:38.792 --> 00:17:40.667
I think it's the
right combination.

00:17:40.667 --> 00:17:43.750
- Sharon, I think,
to John's point,

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:45.750
I think when
you're ultimately confident

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.208
the humility
actually comes with it.

00:17:48.208 --> 00:17:50.667
You know to me, you know listen,

00:17:50.667 --> 00:17:52.291
I think there's
also some benefits

00:17:52.291 --> 00:17:53.417
and I don't want
to speak for John's

00:17:53.417 --> 00:17:55.917
background, but
for me, you know.

00:17:55.917 --> 00:17:57.667
When you're born with so little,

00:17:57.667 --> 00:17:59.166
in a communist country, when you

00:17:59.166 --> 00:18:02.291
come here, I get to watch,

00:18:02.291 --> 00:18:04.583
just watching how my
parents navigated

00:18:04.583 --> 00:18:07.083
the first decade of America,
no complaining,

00:18:07.083 --> 00:18:11.208
working 24/7,
not having a lot of stuff.

00:18:11.208 --> 00:18:13.625
You know it's very just,
it's impossible

00:18:13.625 --> 00:18:16.250
for me to forget
where I came from.

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:17.708
- The same with me.
- And then I would--

00:18:17.708 --> 00:18:19.000
- [Sharon] Yeah,
the same with me.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:20.375
- And then Sharon, I'll tell you

00:18:20.375 --> 00:18:22.458
the other thing that's
really caught my attention,

00:18:22.458 --> 00:18:25.375
presidents, people like
Prince and David Bowie,

00:18:25.375 --> 00:18:27.041
using music references.

00:18:27.041 --> 00:18:30.291
Other athletes,
celebrities, business tycoons,

00:18:30.291 --> 00:18:32.917
you know, you watch them
pass away as you get older and

00:18:32.917 --> 00:18:34.750
you realize you know,
America mourns for

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.750
a day or two, and then
the world moves on, right?

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:38.583
- [Sharon] Right.

00:18:38.583 --> 00:18:40.208
- And so for some reason I got

00:18:40.208 --> 00:18:43.750
all-time legacy
wants and ambitions,

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:46.083
but I also
recognize what I actually

00:18:46.083 --> 00:18:47.667
mean in the
grand scheme of things.

00:18:47.667 --> 00:18:50.291
And that's very
humbling as well.

00:18:50.291 --> 00:18:52.875
- Yeah that's
humbling and I think,

00:18:52.875 --> 00:18:54.125
like he said, when
you know you've come from,

00:18:54.125 --> 00:18:56.083
I think
we're in touch with

00:18:56.083 --> 00:18:59.333
our background
still and we appreciate

00:18:59.333 --> 00:19:00.667
the struggle that
our parents went through

00:19:00.667 --> 00:19:03.542
to get us the opportunities
that we were able to get.

00:19:03.542 --> 00:19:05.166
And we don't take
those things for granted.

00:19:05.166 --> 00:19:06.708
And I don't
forget about people that

00:19:06.708 --> 00:19:08.125
grew up in my
neighborhood,

00:19:08.125 --> 00:19:10.291
and the type of people
that I grew up around.

00:19:10.291 --> 00:19:12.250
When I'm thinking
about where I am now.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.917
Even though I'm in
rarefied air in a lot of ways.

00:19:14.917 --> 00:19:17.625
When I think
about my politics,

00:19:17.625 --> 00:19:19.625
I think about my
philanthropy,

00:19:19.625 --> 00:19:21.542
I'm always thinking
about people that grew

00:19:21.542 --> 00:19:23.250
up in the neighborhoods
where I grew up.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.333
And trying to make
life better for them.

00:19:25.333 --> 00:19:26.959
- Sharon, I'll tell
you one other thing,

00:19:26.959 --> 00:19:28.625
to keep an eye out for,
and this is for everybody.

00:19:28.625 --> 00:19:30.834
I genuinely see,
when I see the cockiest

00:19:30.834 --> 00:19:32.542
or worst versions,
I genuinely feel

00:19:32.542 --> 00:19:35.166
bad for those people.
- It means they're insecure.

00:19:35.166 --> 00:19:36.375
- It's insecurity.
- Yeah.

00:19:36.375 --> 00:19:38.792
- It's a seed of insecurity,
that's what it is.

00:19:38.792 --> 00:19:41.333
Like they're putting up a front,

00:19:41.333 --> 00:19:42.792
because they don't
want you to get through.

00:19:42.792 --> 00:19:44.250
I think of them
as cactuses.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:46.375
They put up that facade,
because if you actually

00:19:46.375 --> 00:19:48.125
get in there,
they're quite weak.

00:19:48.125 --> 00:19:50.000
And I feel like
